⚠️ **IMPORTANT: a superseded `start()` failure still leaks the loser engine**

The generation guard disposes a superseded loser whose `start()` **succeeded** (the checkpoint below disposes + returns), but the `catch` path drops the engine without `dispose()` when the run was superseded (or the view unmounted):

```dart
} on Object catch (error) {
  AppLog.i('apps', 'app start failed: ${widget.app.id} — $error');
  if (mounted && gen == _restartGen) setState(() => _startError = error);
}
```

`engine` is a local here — nobody else can dispose it. Concrete leak shapes, verifiable in `js_app_engine.dart`:

- `JsAppEngine.start()` joins the static sibling group only on success — `_liveByApp.putIfAbsent(app.id, () => <JsAppEngine>{}).add(this)` (js_app_engine.dart:525). A throw **after** the join (e.g. `_replayStorageDrift()` failing) leaves the engine in `_liveByApp[app.id]` forever: it keeps receiving `state.sync` storage broadcasts and holds its native JS context. `dispose()` is the only thing that removes it (js_app_engine.dart:730–737).
- A throw after the inner `JsWidgetEngine` was created (`engine.run(js)` failing mid-IO) leaves the native context unreleased — the exact resource class the `_lifecycleChain` docs (js_app_engine.dart:344–352) say must be explicitly released.

The fix's stated goal is "losers dispose cleanly" — this loser stops one checkpoint short. Since the failed engine is never stored on the view, disposing it in the catch is safe in **all three** cases (superseded, unmounted, current+mounted error card):

```dart
} on Object catch (error) {
  AppLog.i('apps', 'app start failed: ${widget.app.id} — $error');
  // Best-effort: the engine is a local; the view never holds it.
  unawaited(engine.dispose());
  if (mounted && gen == _restartGen) setState(() => _startError = error);
}
```

That also closes the pre-existing variant of the same leak (current+mounted throws never disposed either), not just the superseded one this PR introduces.

💡 **SUGGESTION: the final `_appSignature` write is not generation-guarded**

```dart
setState(() => _engine = engine);
_appSignature = await _readAppSignature();
```

A run that passes the checkpoint above can still be superseded **during** this final read: the loser's late `_appSignature = …` lands after the winner already wrote its own. Both reads usually agree, so the worst case is a spurious/missed `_restartIfAppChanged` that self-heals on the next `fsRevision` bump — cosmetic, hence a suggestion. For symmetry with the rest of the guard:

```dart
if (mounted && gen == _restartGen) {
  _appSignature = await _readAppSignature();
}
```

